# Line endings, declared for the two file types where mixing them costs
# something. The repo is genuinely half and half — measured 2026-08-12 across 531
# tracked text files: 317 CRLF, 209 LF, 5 mixed — and `core.autocrlf=true` keeps
# it that way, because git writes CRLF on checkout while every file this harness
# generates is written LF.
#
# Normalising all 531 would be a 300-file diff for no stated benefit, so this
# names only the two that have one:

# Shell scripts. `install.sh`, `update.sh` and `scripts/restore.sh` — the three a
# new user runs first — are CRLF today, and CI only shellchecks them, never
# executes them. On Linux a CR becomes part of the token (`X=abc\r`) and a CRLF
# shebang breaks direct execution; NOT demonstrated on this machine, because
# Git-for-Windows' bash tolerates both — which is exactly why nobody noticed.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Bridge sources. `scripts/verify-bridges.py` compares the repo copy against the
# one installed in ~/.pi byte for byte, so a `git checkout` that rewrites endings
# reports drift in a file whose content never changed. That happened on
# 2026-08-12 after restoring a leaked mutation, and a drift check that cries wolf
# is a drift check people stop reading.
*.ts text eol=lf
